---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const b = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp');
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Postscript or Mailchimp for cart recovery?',
    answer: 'Choose Postscript when Brands treating SMS as a revenue channel with compliance guardrails. Choose Mailchimp when Teams already on Mailchimp needing Shopify-connected campaigns. Sequenzy still leads as lifecycle layer if neither owns strategy.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Unique AOV test for this pair?',
    answer: 'Use hypothetical $334 and $486 carts to check incentive handling, not to infer product performance. Keep product costs fixed when applying a discount and verify stacking and refund treatment.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Channel escalation?',
    answer: 'Treat the listed delays as hypothetical inputs. For a Mailchimp email recipient, check separate SMS marketing permission before any Postscript branch, assign a queue owner per channel, and recheck purchase and suppression state at dispatch.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Attribution caution?',
    answer: 'Propose a randomized holdout sized for adequate power. Compare contribution after discounts and matched all-in costs; platform attribution alone cannot establish incremental recovery.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Migration tip?',
    answer: 'Map email and SMS queue ownership first. Validate permission, purchase exits and replay deduplication with test events; pause the old sender before enabling its replacement and reconcile queued messages at cutover.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Margin math?',
    answer: '$334 full-price $110.22 vs 10% code $76.82 (Δ $33.4).',
  },
  {
    question: 'Who should choose each?',
    answer: 'Evaluate Postscript for the SMS requirements and Mailchimp for the existing email workflow. Verify current integration and plan support, then choose based on permission handling, queue ownership, purchase exits and matched operating costs; these fixtures establish no winner.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Workflow test?',
    answer: 'Test an email-only subscriber, a separately opted-in SMS recipient, a purchase between queued messages and a replayed cart event. Confirm which sender owns each message and that both queues cancel ineligible sends.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">For a Shopify team evaluating SMS alongside an existing Mailchimp email program, the decision is who owns each recovery message and how both systems receive purchase and suppression updates. The $334 and $486 carts below are hypothetical planning fixtures, not merchant results.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Pick A', tool: a.name, reason: a.tagline }, { label: 'Pick B', tool: b.name, reason: b.tagline }, { label: 'Lifecycle layer', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Strategy and suppression orchestration first.' }]} />
      </div>
      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>{a.name}</th><th>{b.name}</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Brands treating SMS as a revenue channel with compliance guardrails</td><td>Teams already on Mailchimp needing Shopify-connected campaigns</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Usage-based</td><td>Free tier; paid from ~$13/mo</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify depth</td><td>Native</td><td>Integration</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Automation</td><td>Solid</td><td>Basic</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cart channels</td><td>sms, marketing, cart recovery</td><td>email, marketing</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rating</td><td>4.7/5</td><td>4.3/5</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Evaluate the email and SMS handoff</h2>
        <p>Start with a Mailchimp email subscriber who has no documented SMS marketing permission: a cart event must not silently enroll that person in an SMS recovery sequence. Then use a test profile with separate permission for each channel. Assign one queue owner per channel and a shared cart identifier; replay the same event and confirm it does not schedule another message. Verify current plan and integration support for these requirements before selecting either product.</p>
        <p>For the same test cart, simulate a purchase after email is queued but before SMS is dispatched. Check both queues against current purchase and suppression state at dispatch, including when a webhook arrives late. Record which system cancels which message and who handles a failed cancellation. Compare the cost of keeping the existing email setup and adding SMS with the cost of replacing it, using the same audience, message volume, implementation effort and support requirements.</p>
        <p>The scenarios, recovery notes and deep dives below are hypothetical fixtures for checking incentives and exclusions. Their timings, caps and channel choices are test inputs, not observed results or recommended defaults; do not enable a channel without confirming support and purpose-specific permission. Contribution here means revenue less the assumed product cost and discount; deduct delivery, platform, payment, fulfillment and other matched variable costs separately. Repeated fixtures do not demonstrate independent research.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 1 — size matrix with two variants OOS. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 1 favors email proof; watch for recovery for fully OOS cart. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 2 — gift message field abandoned mid-flow. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 2 favors SMS urgency; watch for duplicate SMS and email within 20 minutes. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 3 — high-AOV cart with financing widget opened. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 3 favors push nudge; watch for welcome discount stacking with cart code. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 4 — bundle discount not applied in cart. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 4 favors no-discount assist; watch for discount on email one before trust proof. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 5 — BNPL selected then dropped at review. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 1 favors email proof; watch for SMS after 9pm local without quiet hours. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 6 — B2B cart below MOQ threshold. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 2 favors SMS urgency; watch for no suppression after partial refund. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 7 — logged-in repeat buyer with saved address. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 3 favors push nudge; watch for attribution credit on would-have-returned orders. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 8 — international shipping quote sticker shock. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 4 favors no-discount assist; watch for recovery for fully OOS cart. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 9 — subscription toggle left on at checkout. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 1 favors email proof; watch for duplicate SMS and email within 20 minutes. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
        <p>Postscript vs Mailchimp: scenario 10 — first-time mobile shopper on Instagram traffic. At $334 AOV and 33% margin, full-price recovery contributes $110.22 vs $76.82 after a 10% code (Δ $33.4). Ladder touch 2 favors SMS urgency; watch for welcome discount stacking with cart code. Email 1 ~110m, email 2 ~48h, SMS branch ~7h for opted-in only.</p>
