        <h2>Cart recovery workflow test</h2>
        <p>Keep the existing Mailchimp email sequence as the baseline. For a hypothetical $334 cart, queue email, then record a purchase before the proposed Postscript SMS dispatch. A purchase exit passes only if neither sender sends a recovery message for that completed cart. Repeat with a delayed purchase event: if cancellation cannot be confirmed, keep the second channel paused rather than treating webhook receipt as proof that its queue was cleared. The $486 cart is a separate incentive fixture; a higher cart value alone does not justify a larger discount.</p>
        <h2>Channel escalation</h2>
        <p>Adding SMS gives the team another way to contact a separately eligible shopper, but also adds a second queue, permission record and operating cost. Keep email responsible for the detailed cart explanation and use a proposed SMS branch only for a distinct, useful message. Agree a combined contact budget before enabling it; separate per-tool caps cannot prevent two systems from contacting the same shopper together. The timings in the fixtures below are test inputs, not a recommended schedule.</p>
        <h2>Attribution</h2>
        <p>Propose an email-only baseline against email plus SMS for shoppers eligible for both channels. Randomize at shopper level so repeated carts remain in the same group; size the experiment for adequate power and compare contribution after discounts and matched all-in costs. Platform-attributed revenue does not establish causal incrementality; use the randomized holdout to estimate lift.</p>
        <h2>Retain email or replace the workflow?</h2>
        <p>Retain the current email program when its segments, templates and reporting still meet the team’s requirements and the missing job is a separately permitted SMS branch. Replacing email at the same time makes it harder to distinguish an SMS effect from changes in email content, audience or timing. Consider replacement only when a concrete email requirement is unmet and the replacement has been demonstrated against that requirement; this comparison does not establish that either product can replace the other.</p>
        <p>For a replay test, submit the same cart event twice with the same event identifier, then submit an updated cart with a new event identifier. The first replay should not create another message; the legitimate update should not be discarded merely because the shopper already has a cart record. Keep a ledger of cart ID, event ID, channel, scheduled message ID and cancellation outcome across the two senders. If the team cannot reconcile those records, adding a second sender creates an operational gap even if each individual message looks correct.</p>
        <p>Compare two budgets using the same eligible shoppers and monthly message volume: current email plus proposed SMS, and the proposed replacement workflow. Include platform charges, message and carrier charges where applicable, integration maintenance, template work and the staff time needed to reconcile exceptions. Credit only incremental contribution from the experiment, not the sum of both platforms’ attributed revenue. Retaining email is attractive when it avoids migration work; that advantage can disappear if manual queue reconciliation consumes the saving.</p>
